# Who is asked to review what.
#
# One rule today, and it is deliberately the whole tree.
#
# WHY THERE IS ONLY ONE LINE
#
# Per-area ownership is an OPEN DECISION and is not settled here. The question
# is real - the Go extension contract, the application wiring, the extras and
# the console itself are four surfaces with genuinely different review
# criteria, and the contract binds code that was not written with the core,
# which is the strongest argument for naming a separate reviewer.
# But who reviews which has not been decided, the directories those rules would
# name do not all exist yet, and a CODEOWNERS entry pointing at a path that does
# not exist is silently inert: GitHub does not warn, it simply never matches.
#
# So this file says what is true - everything is the repository owner's - rather
# than inventing an arrangement that would then be read as a decision. When the
# split is decided, it is added here and this comment is replaced by it.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES AND DOES NOT DO
#
# CODEOWNERS requests a review automatically. It only *requires* one where a
# branch protection rule says "Require review from Code Owners", and that
# setting is not available on a private repository under the Free plan. Until
# then this is a routing rule, not a gate - and saying so is the point, because
# a file that looks like enforcement and is not is worse than no file.
#
# SYNTAX NOTE, since it costs a pull request to discover: the LAST matching
# pattern wins, not the most specific one. Any future per-area rule goes BELOW
# the catch-all, never above it.

# Everything.
